I should add that Lojban follows one interesting principle of language 
planning called "facultative precision".

In short, 
1. if you remove words from a Lojbanic utterance you slowly lose precision. 
2. And vice versa, of course (by adding more words you prove that Lojban is 
the most precise language in the world, although, this second aspect of 
Lojban is unfortunately known much more than the 1st aspect).

So e.g. when you remove {da'i} the sentence becomes more vague but it *does 
not get the opposite or another meaning*.

The principle is described in the following article<http://www2.ling.su.se/staff/hartmut/griceil.htm>
.
No wonder they mention Loglan (however, they criticize it based on their 
subjective biased feelings).

